nt="1" source="graph_seed_3" target="graph_paper_5_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_edge_29" value="explicit_pred" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#555555;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="graph_paper_2_1" target="graph_paper_5_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_edge_30" value="explicit_pred" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#555555;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="graph_paper_2_3" target="graph_paper_5_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_edge_31" value="explicit_pred" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#555555;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="graph_seed_3" target="graph_paper_4_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_edge_32" value="explicit_pred" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#555555;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="graph_paper_2_3" target="graph_paper_4_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="graph_edge_33" value="explicit_pred" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#555555;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="graph_paper_2_2" target="graph_paper_4_1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_1" value="2 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_1" target="phase_3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_2" value="3 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_1" target="phase_4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_3" value="1 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_1" target="phase_5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_4" value="2 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_2" target="phase_4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_5" value="2 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_2" target="phase_5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_6" value="2 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_3" target="phase_4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="phase_edge_7" value="1 cross-phase edge(s)" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;dashed=1;endArrow=block;strokeColor=#999999;fontSize=10;" edge="1" parent="1" source="phase_4" target="phase_5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step3" value="Step 3 Graph Serialization + Trend&#10;status: completed&#10;serialized_chars: 40303&#10;trend_chars: 20095" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="3456" width="274" height="114" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step2_step_step3" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step2" target="step_step3"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step3_panel" value="Step 3 Serialization + Trend&#10;Serialized Graph:&#10;# SEED META topic: Research question: Are there more color&#10;pigments to discover? Context: It is possible to create new&#10;pigments, but not new colors. The first synthetic pigment was&#10;made in the early 1700s. In 2009, chemist Mas Subramanian&#10;accidently created a new blue pigment called “YInMn Blue.”&#10;seed_paper: Development of High NIR‐Reflective Red&#10;Li&lt;sub&gt;2&lt;/sub&gt;MnO&lt;sub&gt;3&lt;/sub&gt; Pigments # CITATION SUBGRAPH (17&#10;refs, temporally ordered by year) ## [1] Effect of&#10;hydrothermal treatment on the acidity distribution of γ-Al 2 O&#10;3 support (2006, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth =&#10;1 cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 0 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = True low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 1 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: The paper&#10;addresses how hydrothermal treatment influences the acidity&#10;distribution of γ-Al2O3 supports, which is critical for their&#10;catalytic performance. Existing Methods: Existing approaches&#10;typically characterize γ-Al2O3 acidity after conventional&#10;preparation or calcination, but they provide limited insight&#10;into how hydrothermal modification redistributes acid sites.&#10;Motivation: A better understanding is needed of how&#10;hydrothermal treatment can tune the acid-site distribution of&#10;γ-Al2O3 supports to optimize catalyst support properties.&#10;Proposed Method: The paper proposes applying hydrothermal&#10;treatment to γ-Al2O3 and systematically analyzing the&#10;resulting changes in its acidity distribution. Experiment&#10;Plan: The study likely validates this by preparing&#10;hydrothermally treated γ-Al2O3 samples and comparing their&#10;acid-site populations using characterization methods such as&#10;NH3-TPD, pyridine-IR, and structural analysis. [ABSTRACT] ##&#10;[2] Mn3+ in Trigonal Bipyramidal Coordination: A New Blue&#10;Chromophore (2009, venue=N/A) authors: N/A [EDGE] layer_depth&#10;= 1 cited_in_sections = [] cite_count = 0 section_weight = 0.0&#10;delta_year = unknown is_influential = True low_confidence =&#10;True cited_by_subgraph = 4 [PREDECESSORS] - ref_idx=[]&#10;delta_yr=unknown edge_type=none [IDEA] Problem: The paper&#10;addresses the origin of intense blue coloration in oxides&#10;containing dilute Mn3+ ions in trigonal bipyramidal&#10;coordination. Existing Methods: Existing approaches to oxide&#10;chromophores often rely on empirical color assignments or&#10;conventional coordination environments, without a detailed&#10;electronic-structure explanation for unusual Mn3+ sites. This&#10;limits the ability to understand or design new intense blue&#10;pigments. Motivation: A new approach is needed to connect&#10;local coordination symmetry, electronic transitions, and&#10;optical color in Mn3+-doped oxides. The paper fills this gap&#10;by showing that trigonal bipyramidal Mn3+ is a general and&#10;intense blue chromophore. Proposed Method: The paper combines&#10;optical measurements with first-principles density functional&#10;theory to identify a symmetry-allowed transition from Mn&#10;3d(x2-y2,xy)-O 2p(x,y) valence states to a narrow Mn 3d(z2)&#10;conduction state. It demonstrates this mechanism in a&#10;prototype hexagonal YMnO3-YInO3 solid solution and generalizes&#10;it to diluted Mn3+ in trigonal bipyramidal coordination.&#10;Experiment Plan: The a...&#10;&#10;Trend Summary:&#10;Trends: I treat the seed paper, “Development of High NIR-&#10;Reflective Red Li₂MnO₃ Pigments,” as Paper 0, and the 17&#10;temporally ordered references as Papers 1–17. The subgraph is&#10;branched rather than strictly linear, so the most informative&#10;transitions are both chronological and lineage-based. Paper 0&#10;to Paper 1: The seed’s focus on high-NIR-reflective red&#10;Li₂MnO₃ pigments is only weakly connected to Paper 1, “Effect&#10;of hydrothermal treatment on the acidity distribution of&#10;γ-Al₂O₃ support,” in terms of explicit pigment science.&#10;However, Paper 1 contributes a general materials-chemistry&#10;idea that recurs throughout the later pigment work: controlled&#10;synthesis and post-treatment can redistribute local&#10;surface/structural environments, thereby changing functional&#10;properties. In the pigment context, this logic later appears&#10;as tuning host lattices, coordination environments, and&#10;particle surfaces to control color and stability. Paper 1 to&#10;Paper 2: The field shifts from generic oxide processing to&#10;explicit chromophore discovery and mechanism. Paper 2, “Mn³⁺&#10;in Trigonal Bipyramidal Coordination: A New Blue Chromophore,”&#10;is one of the foundational papers in the graph. It shows that&#10;dilute Mn³⁺ in trigonal bipyramidal coordination produces an&#10;intense blue color, explaining the origin of YInMn-type blue&#10;through optical spectroscopy and first-principles electronic-&#10;structure analysis. This is a major conceptual advance: color&#10;is not treated merely as a compositional outcome, but as a&#10;consequence of local coordination symmetry and allowed&#10;electronic transitions. Paper 2 to Paper 3: The research&#10;expands from Mn³⁺ blue chemistry to Mn⁴⁺ red chemistry. Paper&#10;3, “Li₂MnO₃: a rare red-coloured manganese(IV) oxide&#10;exhibiting tunable red–yellow–green emission,” identifies&#10;Li₂MnO₃ as an unusual red Mn⁴⁺ oxide and attributes its color&#10;and emission to honeycomb-ordered LiMn₆ units in a layered&#10;rock-salt-related structure. This paper becomes the central&#10;foundation for the red Mn-oxide lineage, including the seed&#10;paper’s Li₂MnO₃ pigment work. Paper 3 to Paper 7: This is an&#10;explicit predecessor chain. Paper 7, “Systematic color&#10;variation across the solid solution members, Li₂Mn₁₋ₓTiₓO₃,”&#10;builds directly on Paper 3 by moving from a single red Li₂MnO₃&#10;phase to a full compositional solid-solution series. The&#10;advance is systematic tunability: Mn/Ti substitution is used&#10;to vary color while..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d9eaf7;strokeColor=#5b9bd5;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="3456" width="560" height="1950" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step3_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step3_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step4" value="Step 4 RSS Extraction&#10;status: completed&#10;rss_keys: ['bottlenecks',&#10;'core_gap',&#10;'desired_solution_properties',&#10;'inspiration_search_hints',&#10;'objects',&#10;'possible_mechanism_types',&#10;'relations', 'target_domain',&#10;'unresolved_tensions']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="5506" width="246" height="204" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step3_step_step4" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step3" target="step_step4"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step4_rss_panel" value="Step 4 RSS&#10;target_domain: Pigment materials science, especially discovery&#10;and design of inorganic transition-metal oxide pigments,&#10;organic/inorganic hybrid pigments, NIR-reflective cool&#10;pigments, and responsive thermochromic color materials.&#10;&#10;core_gap: Existing pigment research can explain individual&#10;color cases after synthesis, but it lacks a general predictive&#10;structure-property framework that connects local coordination&#10;environment, dopant oxidation state, lattice distortion,&#10;electronic transitions, powder scattering, stability,&#10;toxicity, and NIR reflectance. Because of this missing&#10;predictive mechanism, the discovery of new pigment materials&#10;remains largely empirical, accidental, or substitution-driven,&#10;and existing methods cannot reliably determine whether a new&#10;host-dopant or hybrid architecture will produce an intense,&#10;stable, non-toxic, and functionally useful color material.&#10;&#10;objects:&#10;- transition-metal color centers such as Mn3+, Mn4+, and Cr3+&#10;- local coordination polyhedra such as trigonal bipyramidal&#10;Mn3+ and distorted MnO6 octahedra&#10;- oxide host lattices such as YInO3-type, Li2MnO3-type,&#10;Li3AlMnO5-type, La3LiMnO7-type, and LaAlO3-type structures&#10;- solid-solution substitution and doping strategies&#10;- d-d electronic transitions and selection-rule relaxation&#10;- visible color metrics such as hue, redness, yellowness,&#10;brightness, and CIELAB parameters&#10;- near-infrared reflectance and solar-thermal behavior&#10;- clay and layered hosts such as palygorskite,&#10;montmorillonite, halloysite, sepiolite, kaolinite, bentonite,&#10;and hydrotalcite&#10;- organic chromophores such as anthocyanins and flavylium&#10;cations&#10;- host-guest confinement and organic/inorganic hybrid pigment&#10;architectures&#10;- thermochromic phase transitions and metastable high-pressure&#10;phases&#10;- particle morphology, scattering, and powder reflectance&#10;- stability factors including photostability, thermal&#10;stability, pH response, and chemical durability&#10;- non-toxicity and sustainable or waste-derived feedstocks&#10;&#10;relations:&#10;- local coordination symmetry controls the allowedness and&#10;intensity of transition-metal optical transitions&#10;- lattice distortion modifies d-d transition energies and&#10;therefore shifts observed hue and color purity&#10;- solid-solution substitution tunes composition, local&#10;structure, and optical absorption simultaneously&#10;- host lattice geometry determines which dopant coordination&#10;environments can be stabilized&#10;- organic chromophore stability depends on confinement,&#10;adsorption, and interaction with inorganic clay hosts&#10;- nanochannel or interlayer confinement can preserve&#10;responsive color change while improving environmental&#10;stability&#10;- macroscopic pigment color depends on both intrinsic&#10;electronic absorption and extrinsic powder scattering&#10;- metastable structural phases can produce irreversible color&#10;change when heating transforms the local transition-metal&#10;environment&#10;- NIR reflectance must be decoupled from visible absorption to&#10;produce cool pigments&#10;- sustainability constraints introduce feedstock variability&#10;that affects phase purity, morphology, and color&#10;reproducibility&#10;&#10;bottlenecks:&#10;- pigment discovery remains empirical because local structure,&#10;electronic transitions, and final powder color are not jointly&#10;predicted&#10;- many intense transition-metal colors rely on weak or&#10;forbidden d-d transitions that require special low-symmetry&#10;environments&#10;- strong visible color often conflicts with high NIR&#10;reflectance, especially for red and orange pigments&#10;- natural organic chromophores remain vulnerable to pH, light,&#10;oxygen, and heat even after clay stabilization&#10;- host-guest pigment design is not generalizable because dye&#10;structure and clay topology must be specifically matched&#10;- thermochromic materials lack unified control over transition&#10;temperature, reversibility, cycling durability, and color&#10;contrast&#10;- waste-derived pigment synthesis struggles with impurity&#10;control, phase reproducibility, and consistent optical&#10;properties&#10;- improving one property such as hue purity does not guarantee&#10;stability, non-toxicity, NIR performance, or scalable&#10;synthesis&#10;&#10;unresolved_tensions:&#10;- intense visible color versus high near-infrared reflectance&#10;- responsive color switching versus long-term chemical and&#10;photostability&#10;- environmentally friendly composition versus high pigment&#10;performance&#10;- metastable phase-enabled thermochromism versus practical&#10;durability and synthesis scalability&#10;- empirical substitution screening versus rational predictive&#10;design&#10;- local electronic color generation versus macroscopic powder&#10;scattering and morphology effects&#10;- natural dye sustainability versus limited intrinsic&#10;stability&#10;- bright color purity versus multifunctional requirements such&#10;as thermal stability, non-toxicity, and cool-coating&#10;performance&#10;&#10;desired_solution_properties:&#10;- predict pigment hue and intensity from composition, host&#10;lattice, dopant state, and local coordination&#10;- identify new pigment candidates without relying solely on&#10;accidental discovery&#10;- achieve strong visible color purity and brightness&#10;- maintain high NIR reflectance for energy-efficient cool&#10;coatings&#10;- provide thermal, photochemical, and environmental stability&#10;- use non-toxic, earth-abundant, or sustainable constituents&#10;- support tunable or reversible color change when dynamic&#10;functionality is required&#10;- accommodate realistic powder morphology and scattering&#10;effects&#10;- enable scalable synthesis with reproducible phase and color&#10;- integrate multiple performance objectives without collapsing&#10;one property to improve another&#10;&#10;possible_mechanism_types:&#10;- coordination-environment engineering to stabilize unusual&#10;color-producing polyhedra&#10;- local-distortion engineering to relax optical selection&#10;rules and intensify d-d transitions&#10;- solid-solution tuning of crystal field, bond distance, and&#10;transition energy&#10;- host-guest confinement mechanisms for stabilizing fragile&#10;organic chromophores&#10;- spectral decoupling mechanisms that separate visible&#10;absorption from NIR absorption&#10;- metastable-phase transition mechanisms for programmable&#10;irreversible or reversible thermochromism&#10;- effective-medium optical modeling that links microscopic&#10;absorption to macroscopic diffuse reflectance&#10;- many-body electronic-structure prediction of transition-&#10;metal multiplet transitions&#10;- inverse design of pigment compositions under multi-objective&#10;constraints&#10;- impurity-tolerant synthesis control for waste-derived or&#10;sustainable pigment feedstocks&#10;&#10;inspiration_search_hints:&#10;- crystal-field and ligand-field design rules from transition-&#10;metal oxide and phosphor chemistry&#10;- computational screening of dopant-host combinations using&#10;DFT, multiplet theory, or dynamical mean-field approaches&#10;- cool-roof and solar-reflective pigment design strategies&#10;that manage visible versus NIR spectral absorption&#10;- Maya Blue-like host-guest confinement principles from clay&#10;mineralogy and dye stabilization&#10;- thermochromic phase-change and metastable materials design&#10;from solid-state chemistry&#10;- inverse materials design and multi-objective optimization&#10;from materials informatics&#10;- scattering-aware optical modeling from pigment, paint, and&#10;powder technology&#10;- encapsulation and nanoconfinement strategies from hybrid&#10;organic-inorganic materials&#10;- waste-to-materials processing principles for impurity-&#10;tolerant pigment synthesis&#10;- non-toxic rare-earth and transition-metal oxide design from&#10;environmentally friendly coating materials" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#e2f0d9;strokeColor=#70ad47;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="5506" width="560" height="3336" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step4_rss_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step4_rss_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step5" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;status: completed&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="8942" width="246" height="90" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step4_step_step5" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step4" target="step_step5"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step5_gate_panel" value="Step 5 Inspiration Radius Gate&#10;use_same_field: True&#10;use_cross_domain: True&#10;preferred_radii: ['R1', 'R2']&#10;same_field_top_k: 2&#10;num_cross_domains: 2&#10;cross_domain_top_k_per_domain: 2&#10;rationale: Same-field retrieval is needed because the RSS and&#10;trend analysis show strong pigment-specific lineages around&#10;Mn/Cr oxide color centers, solid-solution tuning, clay-hosted&#10;hybrid pigments, and NIR-reflective cool pigments. However,&#10;the core gaps are structural and predictive rather than&#10;incremental: linking local coordination and electronic&#10;transitions to powder color, decoupling visible color from NIR&#10;absorption, stabilizing responsive organic chromophores,&#10;programming thermochromism, and handling waste-derived&#10;feedstock variability. These bottlenecks suggest that&#10;mechanism transfer from adjacent domains such as computational&#10;materials screening, phosphor/ligand-field design, cool-&#10;coating spectral engineering, nanoconfinement, and inverse&#10;multi-objective design is valuable." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fce4d6;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="8942" width="560" height="438" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step5_gate_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step5_gate_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step6" value="Step 6 Multi-radius Retrieval&#10;status: completed&#10;candidate_count: 5&#10;round: 3" style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#d5e8d4;strokeColor=#82b366;fontSize=12;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="60" y="9480" width="239" height="96" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step_step5_step_step6" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step5" target="step_step6"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step6_candidates_panel" value="Step 6 Retrieval Candidates&#10;count: 5&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 1&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Exploring the Role of Transition Metal Complexes in&#10;Artistic Coloration through a Bottom-Up Scientific Approach&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Transition metal complexes have historically played&#10;a pivotal role in creating vibrant pigments utilized across&#10;artistic mediums such as ceramics, paintings, and glass&#10;mosaics. Despite their extensive historical use, our&#10;understanding of the mechanisms governing transition met...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 2&#10;domain: same_field&#10;radius: R1&#10;title: Exploring the Role and Variability of 3d Transition&#10;Metal Complexes in Artistic Coloration through a Bottom-Up&#10;Scientific Approach&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Transition metal complexes have historically played&#10;a pivotal role in creating vibrant pigments utilized across&#10;artistic mediums such as ceramics, paintings, and glass&#10;mosaics. Despite their extensive historical use, our&#10;understanding of the mechanisms governing transition met...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 3&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Harnessing surrogate models for data-efficient&#10;predictive chemistry: descriptors &lt;i&gt;vs.&lt;/i&gt; learned hidden&#10;representations&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: When data are scarce, surrogate models trained on QM&#10;descriptors help. We report that their hidden features, not&#10;the predicted descriptors, provide superior inputs for&#10;downstream predictive chemistry tasks.&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 4&#10;domain: computer_science&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Combining Machine Learning and Computational Chemistry&#10;for Predictive Insights Into Chemical Systems&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Machine learning models are poised to make a&#10;transformative impact on chemical sciences by dramatically&#10;accelerating computational algorithms and amplifying insights&#10;available from computational chemistry methods. However,&#10;achieving this requires a confluence and coaction of e...&#10;&#10;candidate_id: 5&#10;domain: physics&#10;radius: R2&#10;title: Computational Studies of the Photophysical, Structural,&#10;and Catalytic Properties of Complex Chemical Systems&#10;plausible: False&#10;abstract: Computational chemistry employs mathematical&#10;algorithms, statistics, and large databases to integrate&#10;chemical theory with experimental observations. Computational&#10;modeling allows us to make predictions concerning molecular&#10;properties and reactivity that ultimately lead to acc..." style="rounded=0;whiteSpace=wrap;html=0;fillColor=#fde9d9;strokeColor=#c55a11;fontSize=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1"><mxGeometry as="geometry" x="360" y="9480" width="620" height="1194" /></mxCell><mxCell id="edge_step6_candidates_panel" value="" style="edgeStyle=none;rounded=0;orthogonalLoop=0;jettySize=0;html=0;endArrow=block;" edge="1" parent="1" source="step_step6" target="step6_candidates_panel"><mxGeometry as="geometry" relative="1" /></mxCell><mxCell id="step_step7" value="Step 7 Analogy Extract